= Esoteric programming language =

== Overview ==
An '''esoteric programming language''' ('''esolang''') is a programming language designed primarily for experimentation, conceptual exploration, artistic or playful expression, unusual constraints, or challenging assumptions about programming rather than ordinary production use.

Esolangs are genuine objects of programming-language design even when practicality is intentionally rejected.

== Motivations ==
Esolangs can explore:

* minimal instruction sets;
* unusual syntax or semantics;
* alternative spatial or temporal execution;
* computational universality;
* self-modification;
* visual or literary representation;
* deliberate difficulty;
* parody and programming culture.

Different languages pursue different goals.

== Examples ==
INTERCAL is an early influential esolang designed against conventional language expectations.

Brainfuck uses an extremely small instruction set.

Befunge uses two-dimensional program layout.

Piet represents programs as images.

Other esolangs may be intentionally noncomputable, conceptual, or impossible to use conventionally.

== Computational power ==
An esolang can be Turing complete, weaker than Turing complete, or have uncertain computational power.

Therefore:

<pre>
esoteric programming language ≠ Turing-complete language by definition
</pre>

== Practicality ==
Lack of production utility is often part of the design.

However, esolangs can still be useful for teaching, language-design research, proof-of-concept constructions, art, puzzles, and studying computation under unusual constraints.

== Programming-language boundary ==
Esolangs expose assumptions often hidden in conventional language design: readability, productivity, notation, control flow, memory, syntax, error handling, and the relationship between source code and human interpretation.

== Related concepts ==
[[Programming language]], [[Turing completeness]], and [[Formal language]] connect esolangs to language design, computational universality, and formal syntax.

